op down with  temperatures as low we're told in  another scene as minus 150 freedom units  or approximately minus 100 degrees  celsius which is cold enough to freeze  helicopter fuel that's not how physics  works right atmospheric physics 101  let's say you have a bunch of air at a  certain temperature how could that  temperature change well you could do two  things add heat to the air or do work on  the air both are important here the heat  one is easier to understand you may have  learned in school but there are three  mechanisms by which heat can be  transferred to an object conduction  convection and radiation conduction is  when stuff touches other stuff and makes  it warmer it's like thermal energy being  directly handed from one substance to  another convection is when warm stuff  moves to colder stuff and mixes with it  the thermal energy is moved by a carrier  and radiation is when stuff eats energy  in the form of electromagnetic radiation  to other stuff which then absorbs it the  energy moves itself as a photon it  doesn't need a carrier in the atmosphere  conduction just isn't a concern air does  not conduct heat well convection however  is what dominates the heat transfer on  short time scales warmer air mixing with  cooler air while oversimplifying  radiation is what dominates on the  longer time scales it's what ultimately  determines climate shouldn't the air  warm up before it reaches ground level  it should but it doesn't the air is  descending too rapidly  if the air were to move extremely  rapidly down through the atmosphere then  as i've already said conduction isn't a  concern and radiation acts on longer  time scales so we could pretty safely  ignore that too conceivably the air  could move in such a way that convection  wasn't significant either if the  descending air mass was particularly  fast and coherent so let's say for the  sake of argument that there's no heat  transfer at all no convection takes  place and the air mass is what we call  thermally isolated but even if the air  in the clip is thermally isolated two  problems firstly the air in the upper  troposphere where they say in the film  the air is from isn't minus 100 degrees  celsius it's probably more like minus 70  degrees celsius at the polar regions  above that the air temperature actually  increases with altitude before gradually  falling again in the mesosphere i've  made a whole video about this for air to  be cold enough to freeze helicopter fuel  it would have to come from the top of  the mesosphere the coldest part of the  atmosphere at about 80 kilometers above  the surface the second problem however  and this is the water flowing uphill  part  has to do with  work as you get higher in the atmosphere  the air pressure decreases this is  because air pressure is just the weight  of air above you and so the higher up  you go the less weight there is above  you and so less pressure that means  however that if a chunk of air were to  descend from the upper atmosphere to the  lower atmosphere it would be moving from  an area of lower pressure to an area of  higher pressure and as air isn't some  solid incompressible thing  that means that the air would be  compressed by that additional pressure  in doing so performs work on the air and  for a system where no heat transfer is  taking place as we've already  established doing work on the system  means the temperature will change  specifically it will increase  we call this adiabatic heating and it's  actually what largely determines the  temperature profile in the lowest part  of the atmosphere as air rises it cools  adiabatically like this note that the  difference between these two curves is  almost entirely just because there's  water in the atmosphere which  kind of makes all the thermodynamics a  bit more complicated so conversely if  you were to take air from the top of the  troposphere and squidge it down to the  surface it wouldn't be very cold in fact  it would be very hot depending on where  you are between 50 and 100 degrees  celsius i can say that with absolute  certainty because the temperature of a  bit of air if you were to squidge it  down to the surface is actually a  variable that we use quite frequently in  atmospheric science we call it the  potential temperature and it's useful  for a variety of calculations we  construct maps like this where you can  see the average potential temperature  with latitude on the horizontal axis and  height on the vertical note that towards  the middle of the stratosphere the  potential temperature is about a  thousand kelvin or 700 degrees celsius  so if you were to somehow drag air down  from higher up in the atmosphere to the  empire state building it wouldn't freeze  it because it's not enough to just  consider the heat transfer in the  atmosphere you also have to think about  the work done in moving a compressible  gas like air from a low pressure  environment to a high pressure  environment near the surface air from  the upper atmosphere then would not  freeze the empire state building it  would roast it and if you took the i  from high enough in the atmosphere would  actually melt it in fact it would not  look too dissimilar from a shot in  another far better roland emmerich movie  picture the scene you're you and you've  just finished watching this video about  applying physics to the movies about how  the world really works and you'd like to  learn more about this but you don't  fancy committing to full-time education  because that's a big financial  commitment and it sounds a bit well  boring well you're in luck because  brilliant this video sponsor is all  about making subjects in science maths  and computer science interesting and  interactive brilliant is a website and  app that improves your scientific and  problem-solving skills by introducing  you to new ideas and then immediately  getting you to apply them the emphasis  here is very much on trying not being  correct after all if 